86045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445382 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fdf03520-5f9c-50d4-9411-5e12d6f34453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1071 "A parking garage initially contains $70$ vehicles. The number of vehicles in the garage $V(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in hours. If the maximum capacity of the garage is $250$ vehicles, which differential equation could be used to model the number of vehicles in the garage? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4108ce6a-5d28-540e-b8c0-e4b52161a458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445383 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the number of vehicles in the garage obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of vehicles changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of vehicles in the garage. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the garage, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=250$ vehicles, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(250-V).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1071 "A high-speed printer in an office building has a maximum printing capacity of $640$ pages per minute; however, current production is below this maximum. Since the technician started monitoring, the printer produced $480$ and $560$ pages per minute at minutes $6$ and $12,$ respectively. What is the printer's printing rate at minute $36?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the printing production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in minutes.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "490a86ce-e0bc-535b-a5a3-da17f507f78c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445384 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate at which the printing production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible printing production and its current printing production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the printer, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=640$ pages, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(640-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{640-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{640-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|640-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $640-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}640-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 640-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 640-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =640-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, at minute $6,$ the printing production was $480$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(6)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(6) & =480 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-6k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, at minute $12,$ the printing production was $560$ pages per minute. So, applying the condition $E(12)=560,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(12) & =560 \\\\ 640-K{e}^{-12k} & =560 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{160}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =2 \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-6\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & =160 \\\\ K & =160{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ K & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=640-320{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of pages printed per minute at minute $t=36$ is\n\n$$\nE(36)=640-320{e}^{-(36/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}=635\\,\\text{pages per minute}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1071 "A bird sanctuary initially has $80$ birds. The number of birds in the sanctuary $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the sanctuary is $110$ birds, which differential equation could be used to model the bird population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "531996ff-0ee7-5943-af5b-93c7fc2f947e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445385 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the number of birds in the sanctuary obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of birds changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of birds in the sanctuary. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the bird sanctuary, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=110$ birds, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(110-B).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1071 "A coastal research station is monitoring the population of brine shrimp in a controlled seawater tank. The tank can sustain a maximum population of $500$ shrimp, but the population is still below this limit. Since researchers began tracking the growth, the population reached $452$ shrimp on day $3$ and $473$ shrimp on day $9.$ On what day will the population reach $490$ shrimp? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of shrimp, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54a5418f-f9b3-5d4d-9779-13b8cf5ffccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445386 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the habitat can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500$ shrimp, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{500-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the population was $452$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(3)=452,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =452 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-3k} & =452 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the population was $473$ shrimp. So, applying the condition $P(9)=473,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =473 \\\\ 500-K{e}^{-9k} & =473 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{48}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} & =48 \\\\ K & =48{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)} \\\\ K & =48\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ K & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ K & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=500-64{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16/9)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$ be the day when the population reaches $490$ shrimp. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =490 \\\\ 500-64{e}^{-kd} & =490 \\\\ 10 & =64{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{10}{64}=\\frac{5}{32} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $490$ shrimp on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{32})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})}=19.3578…≈19,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a1e7865-6d9a-51ad-a7cc-78b26889323e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445387 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P,\\,P(0)=10\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1-0.000\\,05P=0.000\\,05(20\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.000\\,05,a=20\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=10\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=3-0.000\\,4P=0.000\\,4(7,500-P),\\,P(0)=13\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5-0.005P=0.005(1\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=2\\,000\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=13\\,000>a=7\\,500,$ and ${y}_{0}=2\\,000>a=1\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y<200$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9240a0d5-9357-59a5-be58-f932d93ba4fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445388 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.3(200-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and $a=200.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<200$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d00e40d-1dde-5d1e-965b-4da23aadf13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445389 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P,\\,P(0)=31\\,200,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=638-0.02P=0.02(31\\,900-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.02,a=31\\,900,$ and ${y}_{0}=31\\,200.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,216-8P=8(277-P),\\,P(0)=291,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=26-0.005P=0.005(5\\,200-P),\\,P(0)=5\\,600\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=291>a=277$ and ${y}_{0}=5\\,600>a=5\\,200.$" 